# OxyDrop model card

> **Research prototype, not a medical device.** It does not measure SpO2, does not diagnose, and was validated on six hypoxic phone-camera people. The app is built to work on every phone and is not tuned to any specific phone; untested Android and other phones are a limitation (section 12).

This file documents the model that the app runs (section 0: PhotonDistill v2, the round-3 shipped model, numbers from `results/photon/*.csv`) and the previous hybrid engine (sections 1 to 12): architecture, data, training, the leave-one-person-out (LOSO) evaluation, every baseline, every lever (kept and dropped), and an honest comparison with prior work. Every number is generated from `results/ml/*.csv` and `results/photon/*.csv` (see `scripts/ml_numbers.py`, `scripts/render_docs.py`, checked by `scripts/check_claims.py`). The paper-style summary is `WHITEPAPER.md`.

## 0. The shipped model (round 3): PhotonDistill v2

**Why this is new.** Phone-camera low-oxygen detection depends on how far the flash sits from the lens, and the only open phone hypoxia data were recorded on one phone model; we built a leave-one-person-out stress test that simulates a flash farther from the lens than on the recording phone, and trained one small network, PhotonDistill v2, against it, using two simple teachers fitted on clean and physics-degraded copies of the training people only. On clean light it is **on par with** our logistic chain ({{ph:distill2:0}} against {{ph:chain_v1:0}} time-matched balanced accuracy, paired {{ph:distill2:0|d}}, an interval that includes zero; it does not beat it), and under the simulated distant flash it stays above chance where the chain falls to it (out-of-family stress X1: {{ph:distill2:X1}} against {{ph:chain_v1:X1}}), answering fewer windows as the light weakens. The stress test is a simulation, a three-number colour control is nearly as robust, and there are six people, so this is a tested idea, not a validated device. A rerun of the same code on a GPU scored {{ph:distill2_gpu:0}} on clean light against {{ph:distill2:0}} on the locked run, which we report as seed variance (see the seed-variance paragraph below).

**What it is.** One trained student network per seed, the app's default engine (the logistic chain stays behind `?engine=logistic`, the earlier hybrid behind `?engine=hybrid`). Per 10 s window (2 s hop) the app computes {{phw2:n_in}} causal features from the per-frame mean R, G, B, each relative to the person's own label-free first minute: 19 chain deltas (heart-rate rise, ratio-of-ratios, pulse shape), 15 signal-quality features and 5 respiratory features. This front end is unchanged from round 2. A multilayer perceptron {{phw2:n_in}} -> H ReLU -> H ReLU -> 3 heads, with H = {{phw2:hidden}} for the {{phw2:seeds}} seeds ({{phw2:params}} parameters; the seed outputs are averaged), outputs P(SpO2 < 90), an SpO2 estimate (not shown to users) and its own abstain logit; the app answers when the abstain output reaches {{phw2:tau}}. No gate, no teacher and no chain run at inference. Code: `python/oxydrop_ml/photon/distill2.py` (on top of `distill_student.py`), weights `results/photon/distill2_weights.json`, log `results/photon/distill2_log.md`, winner report `results/photon/WINNER3.md`.

![PhotonDistill v2 architecture](docs/figures/photon_architecture.png)

**Training** (inside each outer fold, on the five training people only). Two teachers are refit in every outer and inner fold: logistic chain v1 gives soft targets on the clean windows, and a DC-colour logistic regression (three baseline-relative ln DC values) gives soft targets on physics-sampled distant-flash copies of the training recordings (FlashGap's declared augmentation prior: flash-lens offset, light leak, noise, exposure gain; its own random stream, disjoint from the stress levels and never X1). Loss: label cross-entropy plus a tempered distillation term plus Huber on SpO2 plus a cross-entropy abstain head that predicts whether the hypoxia head is right. Round 3 widened round 2's grid in a two-stage nested search on leave-one-inner-person-out CV: teacher set (base, or base averaged with a gradient-boosted tree teacher), distillation weight (8, 16, 32 or pure distillation), temperature (2 or 4), then width H (16, 32, 64) and weight decay, plus epochs and the abstain threshold. Over the {{d2fold:n}} seed folds the inner search chose the base teachers {{d2fold:teacher:base}} times (the GBDT teacher was never chosen), distillation weight 16 in {{d2fold:lam:16}} and 32 in {{d2fold:lam:32}} folds (pure distillation never), and width 64 in {{d2fold:H:64}}. So the clean-light gain over round 2 comes from the larger distillation weight, the temperature and the width, not from a new teacher. The deployed weights are the three seeds refit on all six people with the modal nested recipe of each seed.

**How it was chosen.** The round-3 rule was fixed at 19:55 CT before any round-3 score (Amendment 8, `.agents/tasks/task-oxydrop-photon-model/ROUND3.md`): replace round 2's PhotonDistill only if a candidate's clean-light point estimate is higher, its paired difference to PhotonDistill has a lower bound above -0.02, and its mean paired stress difference to the chain over L3, L4, L5 and X1 is at least 0, with null controls passed. PhotonDistill v2 meets all four: {{ph:distill2:0}} against {{ph:distill:0}}; paired to PhotonDistill {{phd:distill2:0}} ({{phd:distill2:0|nb}} people better); stress mean {{w3:distill2:g3_mean}}; nulls mean {{phnull:distill2}}. The planner locked it at 20:22 CT (`results/photon/WINNER3.md`).

**Clean light (L0) against every baseline and every candidate of all three rounds** (`results/photon/TABLE.csv`, evalkit, 95% person-bootstrap intervals, paired differences to chain v1 on the same resampled people). This harness re-runs the bootstrap, so chain v1 reads {{ph:chain_v1:0|full}} here and {{b1:chain_v1:tm_bacc|full}} in section 8 (same model, other draws). The shipped pipeline R ({{hy:default_R:tm_bacc|full}}, section 8) is optimistic because its rule was chosen after the outer scores were seen; the CNN alone is {{ab:full/p23:p1_tm_bacc|full}} in the ablation section and reads {{ph:cnn_full:0}} on this harness.

{{@photon_candidates}}

Not in the table: `ssl3` produced no score file. `ens3`, a stacking ensemble of PhotonDistill, OxySeq and the no-teacher student (three models, so not one shippable network), landed after the lock with no null controls; `results/photon/WINNER3.csv` scores it at {{w3:ens3:tm|full}}, paired to chain v1 {{w3:ens3:d_chain}} ({{w3:ens3:nb_chain}} people better). It is a candidate only.

![Clean-light forest plot](docs/figures/photon_results_ci.png)

**The plain statement: the shipped trained model is on par with the logistic chain on clean light and does not beat it** (paired {{ph:distill2:0|d}}, {{ph:distill2:0|nb}} people better, worst person {{ph:distill2:0|worst}} against the chain's {{ph:chain_v1:0|worst}}). "Beats the chain" needs the interval above 0 and at least 5 of 6 people better; neither holds. The round-2 model it replaces lost to the chain by {{ph:distill:0|dabs}} (paired {{ph:distill:0|d}}).

**Seed variance: the locked run and the GPU rerun.** The locked run (CPU, Daytona) scored {{ph:distill2:0|full}} on clean light, paired to the chain {{ph:distill2:0|d}}. After the lock we reran the same code with the student network on a Kaggle T4 GPU (`results/photon/distill2_gpu_log.md`; same teachers, grid, seeds, batch order and initial weights): {{ph:distill2_gpu:0|full}}, paired {{ph:distill2_gpu:0|d}}, {{ph:distill2_gpu:0|nb}} people better, worst person {{ph:distill2_gpu:0|worst}}. The three GPU seeds score {{ph:distill2_gpu_s0:0}}, {{ph:distill2_gpu_s1:0}} and {{ph:distill2_gpu_s2:0}}. We report the difference as seed variance: GPU and CPU float32 sums accumulate in a different order, which over up to 30 epochs of Adam changes which grid point wins the inner search in 5 of 18 seed-fold searches (the other 13 match, with slightly different trained weights). The two scores are therefore two draws of the same method on six people, and the clean-light comparison with the chain is on par on the locked run and slightly below on the rerun (its paired interval is {{ph:distill2_gpu:0|d}}), and neither run beats the chain. On the stress levels the runs agree: X1 {{ph:distill2_gpu:X1}} on the GPU against {{ph:distill2:X1}} on the locked run. The GPU weights were not swapped into the app, and the scores of the shipped model in this document are the locked run's.

**Clean and simulated distant-flash stress side by side** (TM [95% CI]; second line: paired difference to chain v1 [95% CI], verdict, people better). Levels L1 to L5 add 2 to 10 mm of simulated flash-to-lens offset to the held-out person's whole recording; X1 is an out-of-family degradation that no training path samples. No model is refit per level. PhotonDistill v2 wins against the chain at L3 ({{ph:distill2:3|d}}), L4 ({{ph:distill2:4|d}}, {{ph:distill2:4|nb}}), L5 ({{ph:distill2:5|d}}) and X1 ({{ph:distill2:X1|d}}, {{ph:distill2:X1|nb}}).

{{@photon_stress}}

![Stress curve](docs/figures/photon_stress_curve.png)

**Abstention.** Abstention is the model's own abstain head with a nested threshold. As the simulated light weakens, PhotonDistill v2 answers fewer windows and keeps most of its answered accuracy, while the chain keeps answering at about chance and OxySeq's learned abstain head does not notice the low light:

{{@photon_abstain}}

**Disclosures that apply to every number in this section.**

* All round-3 models were designed after the round-1 and round-2 outer results on the same six people were seen (Amendment 8, `python/oxydrop_ml/amendments/AMENDMENT_8_round3.md`); round-2 models after round 1 (Amendment 7). PhotonDistill v2's wider grid was chosen because round 2's distillation weight hit the grid edge in every fold. Their clean-light numbers are optimistic and not a blind test, and picking the round-3 winner by its clean-light point estimate adds a selection effect that is not quantified.
* **Deadline.** PhotonDistill v2's clean-light ensemble arrived at 20:08 CT; its null and stress files arrived at 20:16 CT, one minute after the stated 20:15 CT cutoff. The winner script flags this as late; the planner's lock accepted it and the waiver is written next to the flag in `results/photon/WINNER3.md`.
* The stress test is a simulation that scales whole recordings. Features relative to the person's first minute cancel a constant attenuation by design, so part of the robustness is built in. The control `ctl_dc_lr` (logistic regression on three baseline-relative ln DC values, no network) is nearly as stress-robust ({{ph:ctl_dc_lr:X1}} at X1, a tie with chain v1 by its interval). Levels L1 to L5 lie inside the augmentation prior (in-simulator); only X1 is out of family. Real phones with a distant flash are untested.
* Null control (labels permuted across the training windows, teachers included): {{phnull:distill2|draws}}, mean {{phnull:distill2}} over {{phnull:distill2|n}} draws (the rule asks for a mean of at most 0.55).
* Teachers: the GBDT-augmented teacher was offered and never chosen; the physics teacher (PhotonTwin inversion) was not used because it needs raw per-frame RGB and the optics renderer per fold, which did not fit the deadline; OxySeq and OxyTrack outputs were not used as teachers because their saved scores would leak the held-out person.
* One dark-skinned person among the six Hoffman people: no skin-tone claim is made. Six people, 12 recordings, Nexus 6P phones.
* The GPU rerun of PhotonDistill v2 (`distill2_gpu`, files `results/photon/distill2_gpu_*`) arrived after the planner's lock and is reported only as the seed-variance check above. Its null draws are {{phnull:distill2_gpu|draws}}, mean {{phnull:distill2_gpu}}; one draw is individually above the 0.55 flag level, while the mean passes the rule.
* Other candidates are reported as candidates, not results: MultiDA loses on clean light ({{ph:multida:0|d}}); PhotonNet-XL loses ({{ph:photonnet_xl:0|d}}); round 2's PhotonNet and OxySSL fail their null controls ({{phnull:photonnet}}, {{phnull:oxyssl}}); OxySeq loses on clean light and its abstain head does not detect the simulated low light; the round-1 models (Amendment 6) include `twin_gate`, a hand-coded gate that the round-2 requirement ruled out, and OxyTrack, whose clean-light tie is optimistic because four versions were scored on the outer folds.

## 1. The previous engine (hybrid, `?engine=hybrid`), in one paragraph

Sections 1 to 12 document the hybrid that was the app's engine before round 2 and its pre-registered P1 to P3 evaluation on `results/ml`.


A **hybrid of a physiological logistic chain and a small 1D CNN**. The chain (the v1 model: heart-rate rise, pulse-shape and ratio-of-ratios features, each relative to the person's own first minute, two logistic regressions) gives a low-oxygen logit. A {{v:params}}-parameter CNN (`OxyNet`, three seeds) learns a **residual correction** to that logit from the 10 s window of per-frame mean RGB. A small logistic meta-learner maps the sum to a decision. The same CNN also outputs heart rate, a quality score and an experimental SpO2 value that the app never shows. **The pre-registered primary metric was not beaten**: the deployed pipeline R scores {{hy:default_R:tm_bacc|full}} (optimistic: chosen after the outer scores were seen) and the nested per-person-selected hybrid {{hy:HYBRID_FINAL:tm_bacc|full}}, both ties with the shipped chain ({{b1:chain_v1:tm_bacc|full}}). **A chain refit with the same levers and no CNN has the higher point estimate ({{hy:CHAIN_SAME_LEVERS:tm_bacc}}), and so does the chain-only conformal control (the conformal section)**; the intervals overlap, so this is a tie. ML is the app's core path by design (Amendment 2), not because it wins.

![Architecture](docs/figures/ml_architecture.png)

![Input to output](docs/figures/ml_flow.png)

## 2. Who it is for and input to output

People with COVID-19, COPD, asthma or at altitude who own a phone but no pulse oximeter, and low-resource clinics. **Input:** 30 to 60 s of fingertip video (rear camera and flash), reduced on the phone to per-frame mean R, G, B and frame timestamps. **Output:** every 2 s after a 60 s label-free calibration, one of *Normal vs your baseline*, *Possible low oxygen: re-check with a pulse oximeter*, *Unsure, re-measure*; plus heart rate and a quality score. No pixel leaves the device.

## 3. Architecture and parameters

`python/oxydrop_ml/model.py` (specification: `python/oxydrop_ml/MODEL_SPEC.md`). **Parameters: {{v:params}}** shipped (budget 300,000, asserted in a test); about 11.6 million multiply-adds per window; one member's JSON is {{v:member_kb}} (three seed members {{v:members_mb}}) and the ONNX interoperability file is {{v:onnx_kb}}. A single forward pass takes {{v:deploy_fwd_ms}} ms per 10 s window in Node on a development laptop (feature construction 0.2 to 1 ms) and a full hybrid window (v2 quality chain, v1 chain, features, three forward passes, combiner) {{v:deploy_win_ms}} (`results/ml/DEPLOY.md`, laptop numbers). Phones are slower by an unmeasured factor; the app prints the real latency per window in its debug view, so every phone test gives one. The three seed files are {{v:deploy_mb}} together.

| Stage | Detail |
|---|---|
| Input `x` | 10 channels by 300 samples (10 s at 30 Hz) and a 9-value side vector |
| Stem | one `Conv1d(1,8,k5)` shared by the R, G, B waveform slots (masked per domain) plus 7 auxiliary channels, `Conv1d(31,24,k5)`, plus a per-domain embedding (phone, contact oximeter) |
| Body | 4 stages of 2 residual blocks, widths 24, 40, 56, 80, kernel 3, dilations 1 and 2, stride 2 at stages 2 to 4 (T = 300, 150, 75, 38), BatchNorm folded at export |
| Pooling and embedding | global average and max (160 values), `Linear(160,64)` with ReLU |
| Heads | HR: 141 bins over 40 to 180 bpm; SpO2 and log-variance (experimental); low-oxygen logit on (embedding minus the person's baseline embedding) plus the side vector; signal-quality logit (will the HR be within 5 bpm?); a training-only auxiliary SaO2 head for the contact-oximeter domain, never exported |

**Input channels** (`python/oxydrop_ml/features.py`): 0 to 2 `ac_R/G/B` (100 times the causal 0.7 to 4 Hz band-pass of ln(rgb + 1)); 3 frame-time delta (0 when frames arrive on time); 4 and 5 per-beat ln ratio-of-ratios R/G and R/B minus the person's baseline value (the physics channels); 6 beat mask; 7 to 9 slow log DC minus the person's first-minute log DC. **Side vector:** baseline-relative ratio-of-ratios, DC and local amplitude changes and beat fraction. **Causality:** every filter is single-pass (no zero-phase filtering) and the baseline is the person's own first 60 s, label-free, so the JavaScript port in `app/js/` computes the same tensor on the fly (feature parity to 1e-6, network parity to 1e-4 on fixtures).

**Losses** (`python/oxydrop_ml/losses.py`, each head contributes only where its label exists): soft-label cross-entropy over the HR bins; Gaussian negative log-likelihood plus Huber for SpO2 (log-variance clamped); class-weighted BCE for SpO2 < 90 (weight 3 for the shipped recipe); BCE for quality; Huber for the contact-oximeter SaO2 head; an optional gradient-reversal domain head (ablation only).

## 4. Training data and licences

| Source | Use | Licence | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| UW Hoffman 2022 | P1 test (6 people, {{v:n_windows_p1}} windows), low-oxygen training in each fold | MIT | the only open phone-camera data with SpO2 below 90 |
| MTHS (Samavati et al.) | P2 and P3 test, normoxic phone video | CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 | non-commercial research; weights trained on it are shared for non-commercial research and education use only, with attribution (`DATA_LICENSES.md`, `app/data/WEIGHTS_LICENSE.md`); no raw data or per-window predictions committed |
| BUT PPG v2.0 | P2 test, smartphone PPG with ECG heart rate | CC BY 4.0 | spot SpO2 per person used only as a low-weight label |
| OpenOximetry v1.1.1 | SSL, stage B warm-up, auxiliary head, skin-tone analysis | PhysioNet credentialed | contact red/infrared PPG, not a phone; derived aggregates only; Daytona only, never Kaggle; {{v:ssl_excluded}} people held out of everything |
| Pretraining corpus | SSL: {{v:ssl_rows}} windows, {{v:ssl_subjects}} people (OpenOximetry, PTT-PPG, moshekam exercise, apnoea, BIDMC) | mixed | disjoint from every Hoffman, MTHS and BUT person |

## 5. Self-supervised pretraining

SimCLR-style contrastive learning (PPG-appropriate augmentations), {{v:ssl_steps}} steps; checkpoint SHA-256 prefix `{{v:ssl_sha}}`.

{{@ssl}}

![SSL curves](docs/figures/ml_ssl_curves.png)

A Kaggle pretraining on HealthRing PPG (different 5x450 network, predictions only) gave SpO2 MAE {{v:hr_pre_mae}} pretrained, {{v:hr_scr_mae}} from scratch, {{v:hr_frz_mae}} frozen: pretrained against scratch is a tie, and it cannot initialise OxyNet, so it is not used.

## 6. Device augmentation

Per-channel gain and gamma, colour-matrix jitter, DC steps, baseline wander, frame-rate jitter and drops, resampling phase, saturation clipping, motion bursts, finger-pressure drift (`python/oxydrop_ml/augment.py`). **The device-agnostic augmentation novelty claim is not supported**: removing it changes P1 by {{ab:no_aug/p23k:p1_tm_bacc}} against {{ab:full/p23:p1_tm_bacc}} for the full model (inside the seed spread {{ab:full/p23:p1_tm_bacc|seedsd}}). No augmentation parameter or threshold is fit to any phone.

## 7. Evaluation protocol (pre-registered, nested)

`python/oxydrop_ml/PREREGISTRATION.md` was committed in `{{v:prereg_commit}}` before any learned-model result and has five amendments (Amendment 1 in the pre-registration file, 2 to 5 in `python/oxydrop_ml/amendments/`, each stating what had been seen when it was written).

* **LOSO everywhere**; both hands of a person held out together; no window-level split. Rows that are not LOSO (ablation P2 and P3 use 10 grouped folds) are labelled in the protocol column.
* **Nested CV for every selection** (recipe, hyperparameters, thresholds, stacking, which lever to keep, conformal quantiles): an inner subject-grouped CV on the outer training people only. Disclosed deviation (Amendment 1): the single CNN recipe was taken over all outer folds and its validation people include P2 and P3 test people; the winner never changes when any one person is dropped.
* **Pretraining only on people who are never a test person.**
* **Seeds:** three for every final CNN row; seed SD reported.
* **Intervals:** 95% person-level bootstrap (2000 resamples of people); paired person bootstrap for differences. A *win* is claimed only against the **best** honest baseline.
* **Gap:** fold models scored on their own pooled training people against the pooled held-out score.
* **Primary metrics:** **P1** Hoffman time-matched balanced accuracy at full coverage; **P2** share of windows within 5 bpm of the reference at full coverage (BUT PPG plus MTHS, 100 people); **P3** SpO2 MAE on MTHS ({{v:n_people_p3}} people with a scored window).

## 8. Results

### 8.1 P1: low-oxygen detection (primary)

{{@p1_main_full}}

Majority class is {{v:majority_share}} of the windows. *Per person* = mean ± SD of the six held-out people. The pre-registered rule applies to the **best** baseline, the shipped v1 chain ({{b1:chain_v1:tm_bacc|full}}; accuracy {{b1:chain_v1:acc|full}}).

**Reading.** The CNN ties the chain (3 seeds {{cp:P1/0/chain_v1|cnn}}, {{cp:P1/1/chain_v1|cnn}}, {{cp:P1/2/chain_v1|cnn}}; seed 1 alone loses, {{cp:P1/1/chain_v1|d}}; the ensemble {{cp:P1/-1/chain_v1|cnn}} ties, {{cp:P1/-1/chain_v1|d}}). The nested hybrid ties it too ({{pd1:HYBRID_FINAL/chain_v1|d}}), and so does the deployed pipeline R ({{hy:default_R:tm_bacc|full}}, no paired test run; this is the model the app runs and its estimate is optimistic because R was chosen after the outer scores were seen). A chain refit with the same nested levers reaches {{hy:CHAIN_SAME_LEVERS:tm_bacc|full}}, a higher point estimate than either hybrid, so any gain over the shipped chain is a recalibration, not a CNN effect. The only systematic wins are against weak baselines (ratio-of-ratios, clock), which are not pre-registered wins.

